 ## Background
-Scaffold-GS was proposed as an advancement in 3D Gaussian Splatting to address limitations in initialization and memory efficiency [S1]. It builds on the idea of using neural representations to predict Gaussian attributes, similar to NeRF-like approaches but adapted for Gaussian splatting [S1]. It has influenced subsequent methods like Octree-GS, which introduces level-of-detail strategies based on the Scaffold-GS backbone [S1].
+Scaffold-GS was proposed as an advancement in 3D Gaussian Splatting to improve upon the original method's limitations in initialization and attribute representation. It builds on the idea of using voxel grids to store features, which are then used to predict Gaussian attributes via lightweight neural networks [S1]. This approach is distinct from other variants that modify the kernel function or use different sorting mechanisms [S1].
 
